=== Step 4: Analysis -- query performance vs write throughput ===
  Write cost. Inserting 10,000 products took 0.085s (median of 5 runs) with only the _id
  index and 0.335s with 5 secondary indexes: a +294.8% slowdown (117,777 -> 29,831
  docs/sec). Every insert has to add an entry to every index, and the multikey tags index
  and the text index on name add several entries per document. idx_stock served none of
  the 5 queries, yet every write paid for it. With only idx_category_price_rating kept,
  the same insert took 0.119s (+40.4% vs no indexes).

  Read cost. After dropping to idx_category_price_rating, Q1, Q5 still used an index,
  while Q2, Q3, Q4 fell back to a COLLSCAN that examines all 20,000 documents (Q2 993 ->
  20,000 docs examined, 6.1 -> 12.8 ms; Q3 4,184 -> 20,000 docs examined, 20.9 -> 20.2 ms;
  Q4 478 -> 20,000 docs examined, 4.7 -> 15.3 ms). Wall-clock time moves less than
  documents examined because, at 20,000 documents, much of each query's time is sending
  its matches back to the client. Documents examined is the gap that grows with the
  collection: a COLLSCAN reads every document, an index scan reads only the matching keys.

  Trade-off. Indexes move cost from reads to writes. A read-heavy product catalogue should
  keep the indexes its frequent queries actually use -- $indexStats shows which -- and
  drop the ones nothing reads, like idx_stock here. A write-heavy workload such as logs or
  sensor data should keep the fewest indexes that still meet its read latency targets. One
  index that serves several queries, as idx_category_price_rating does for Q1 and Q5,
  gives the most read benefit per unit of write cost.

  Timings vary from run to run; re-run to check the numbers are stable.

